{"contract_version":"akw-js-runtime-v1","ts":"2026-08-18T12:02:44.896Z","type":"workflow.log","phase":"Bundle-And-Vectorize","message":"summary: Dependency-scheduled SIMD/VLIW kernel validated on three independently seeded forest_height=10, rounds=16, batch_size=256 runs. Both output values and output indices matched the reference. It emits 2,139 cycles, uses 1,259 of 1,536 scratch words, and improves the 147,734-cycle baseline by approximately 69.1x. The implementation intentionally requires batch_size to be divisible by VLEN; this matches the specified batch_size=256 workload and avoids unsafe partial-vector stores."}
{"contract_version":"akw-js-runtime-v1","ts":"2026-08-18T12:02:44.896Z","type":"workflow.log","phase":"Bundle-And-Vectorize","message":"bundling: build(slots, vliw=True) is the sole multi-slot bundle constructor. build_kernel first represents constants, input loads, gathers, hashes, index updates, wrapping, and output stores as a dependency DAG. A topological list scheduler selects only tasks ready at the beginning of each cycle, fills up to load=2, valu=6, alu=12, store=2, and flow=1 slots, and passes that independent ready set to build. This explicitly respects the simulator rule that writes become visible only at the end of a cycle and prevents same-bundle producer-consumer hazards."}
{"contract_version":"akw-js-runtime-v1","ts":"2026-08-18T12:02:44.896Z","type":"workflow.log","phase":"Bundle-And-Vectorize","message":"vectorization: The 256 inputs are represented as 32 vectors of eight lanes. Index and value vectors are loaded once and remain resident in scratch for all 16 rounds, eliminating intermediate round stores and reloads. Each forest lookup uses a vector address calculation followed by eight load_offset gather slots; the global scheduler overlaps those two-slot gathers with SIMD work from other groups. Hash stages 0, 2, and 4 are algebraically reduced to one multiply_add each. The remaining stages use two parallel SIMD transforms followed by a combine. Branch selection is computed as 2*idx + 1 + hash%2, and wrapping is implemented as idx*(idx<n_nodes), so the round body uses no flow slots."}
