{"contract_version":"akw-js-runtime-v1","ts":"2026-08-19T11:31:40.987Z","type":"workflow.log","phase":"Bundle-And-Vectorize","message":"summary: This version keeps the required KernelBuilder interface, replaces one-slot bundles with a dependency-aware VLIW scheduler, and gives each batch lane private scratch registers so independent lanes can fill up to 12 ALU, 2 load, 2 store, and 1 flow slot per cycle. It uses flow add_imm for contiguous input addressing, reuses the gathered node register, deduplicates hash constants, and preserves debug ordering and pause behavior."}
{"contract_version":"akw-js-runtime-v1","ts":"2026-08-19T11:31:40.987Z","type":"workflow.log","phase":"Bundle-And-Vectorize","message":"bundling: The build() method greedily forms each cycle by scanning pending operations until the first debug barrier. It tracks scratch reads and writes for ALU, VALU, load, store, and flow instructions, rejects same-cycle producer-consumer and write-write hazards, respects every engine slot limit, and removes selected operations while retaining original dependency order. Debug comparisons remain separate zero-cycle bundles so they observe end-of-cycle committed values."}
{"contract_version":"akw-js-runtime-v1","ts":"2026-08-19T11:31:40.987Z","type":"workflow.log","phase":"Bundle-And-Vectorize","message":"vectorization: The forest lookup is an indexed gather: the ISA exposes vload only for contiguous addresses and provides no scalar extraction or gather instruction. A fully vectorized lane path would therefore require changing the computation or adding unsupported operations. The implementation intentionally preserves correctness by vectorizing only where the ISA can express the operation safely; the main realized optimization is cross-lane VLIW parallelism around the unavoidable scalar gather and data-dependent hash."}
